WEBVTT

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:09.010
Bernard, Minister, not in yet?

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.330
Oh, yes. He's in his office having a meeting.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:12.050
What about?

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.270
Nothing very important.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.140
I see. And what was that meeting in there yesterday?

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.780
The Minister was just reviewing procedures for briefing him on answers to parliamentary questions.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:29.200
But there were principals present and assistant secretaries and other assorted underlings.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.560
Just the ones who actually supply him with the actual information, actually, Sir Humphrey.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.480
Bernard, this has to be stopped at once.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.100
Why?

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.340
Well, if he talks to the underlings, he may learn things that we don't know.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.880
Our whole position could be undermined.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.780
But why, if it increases our knowledge?

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.240
It is folly to increase your knowledge at the expense of your authority.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:50.870
Why?

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.840
Bernard, please stop saying why.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:54.920
Why?

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.580
I mean, could you develop that point, please?

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.620
Would you say that the minister is starting to run the department?

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.840
Oh, yes, indeed. Actually, things are going pretty well, actually.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.880
No, Bernard. When a minister actually starts to run his department,

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.740
things are not going pretty well, actually.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.240
They're actually going pretty badly.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.360
But actually, I mean, in fact,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.380
isn't it the minister's job to run the department?

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.900
No, Bernard, it is our job.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.480
Or, to be more precise, it is my job,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.440
for which I've had 25 years' experience and training.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:29.970
Don't you realise what...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.790
would happen if we allowed the minister to run the department no what well in

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.700
the first place there would be chaos naturally and in the second which is

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.110
much more serious there would be innovations changes Bernard public

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:48.710
debate outside scrutiny gosh is that what you want good heavens no I mean but

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:53.770
what should he do then Bernard a minister has three functions first as

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.340
an advocate making the department's actions seem plausible to Parliament

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.960
and public. He is in fact our public...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.740
relations man. Second, he is our man in Westminster, steering our legislation

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:11.270
through Parliament. And third, he is our breadwinner. He has to fight in cabinet

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:17.010
for the money we need to run our department. But he is not here to review

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.790
departmental procedures with principals and assistant secretaries. What if he's

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.790
got the time? Why has he got the time? He shouldn't have the time. That's your

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:29.840
fault, Bernard. You're here to ensure that he hasn't got the time. Create activity.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.000
You should be making speeches, provincial visits.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.130
We need deputations, junkets abroad, mountains of red boxes.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.310
We need crises, emergencies, panics.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.510
Pull yourself together, Bernard.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.570
You must be leaving spaces in his diary.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:45.530
No, he makes those.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:46.570
Well, fill them up again.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:47.390
Well, he won't let me.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.890
Don't ask him. Do it!

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.470
Make sure he spends more time where he can't get under our feet

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.530
and can't do any damage.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.050
But where?

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:56.620
Well, the House of Commons, for instance.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.860
Look, Sir Humphrey, I really do try my best.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.320
There's a deputation in there at the moment.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.520
Well, what is it about, Bernard?

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.200
Well, something completely trivial.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.600
Preserving badgers in Warwickshire.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:10.980
Well done, Bernard.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.960
Why didn't you tell me this when I asked you before?

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.340
Well, actually, I thought it was rather, well, trivial.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.900
A waste of the Minister's time.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.740
I didn't think you would approve, actually.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:22.760
Burn.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.370
Oh, well, I'll try and find some more threatened species then, shall I?

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:29.970
Well, you may not say...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.960
to look very far. The private secretaries who can't occupy their ministers are a threatened species.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:45.970
Actually. Don't you see? We have a sacred trust to preserve our natural heritage. But that's

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:50.190
exactly what this new legislation is designed to ensure. As I said in the house yesterday,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:55.170
the system at the moment simply isn't working. It's a hodgepodge. Local authorities,

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:59.610
tourist authorities, national parks, countryside commission, CPR.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.200
Nobody knows where they are.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.120
Nothing gets done.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.760
And they're all bat-biting and butt-passing.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.120
I mean, you know what committees are like.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:07.500
Oh, we're a committee.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:08.810
Yes.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.240
Didn't mean your sort of committee,

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.440
but this new legislation is designed to create clear authority

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.740
and simple procedures,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.060
and incidentally save a great deal of public money.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:19.960
You ought to welcome it.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.300
Yes, that's all very well.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.080
But what about Hayward Spinney?

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.100
Well, a few very small areas

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.240
will have to lose their specially protected status

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.780
because it simply isn't economical any longer.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.860
to maintain them as they ought to be maintained.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.840
But Hayward Spinney is a vital part of Britain's heritage.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.560
I mean, the badgers have dwelt there for generations.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.300
Ah, well, now, can you be sure of that?

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:40.720
It said so in The Guardian.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:41.640
Oh, no.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.690
Actually, what it says here is that the bodgers have dwelt there for generations.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:53.870
Well, how would you feel if you were going to have a lot of office blocks

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.490
built over your garden by a lot of giant badgers?

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.200
But a lot of office blocks are giant badgers.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.710
There's nothing special about man, Mr. Hacker.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.320
We're not above nature.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:05.720
We're all part of it.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:07.860
Men are animals too, you know.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:08.800
I know that.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:10.040
I've just come from the House of Commons.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.100
Sorry to interrupt you, Minister,

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.960
but you are due for your next appointment.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.120
Oh, what a pity.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.920
Well, thank you anyway for putting your case so persuasively.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:20.940
It's been a great pleasure.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.380
If you go down the corridor,

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:23.760
I'll turn there to the end and down the stairs.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.760
But what are you going to do about it?

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.860
All views will be taken into consideration

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:29.960
at the appropriate time.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:38.980
now if you'll excuse me very polished performance minister gracious emollient

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.740
and said absolutely nothing very professional why wasn't I warned warned

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.480
about what minutes why wasn't I won the unifying the administration of the

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.380
countryside would mean removing protected status from this beaver what's a badger

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.200
colony minister Haywood spinney East Warwickshire somebody must have known

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.760
about it surely well if somebody did happen to know minister they wouldn't

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.720
necessarily know that you wanted to know what...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.960
What they happen to know, in case...

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.360
Ah, Sir Humphrey.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:06.880
Good morning, Minister.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.760
Humphrey, do you see it as part of your job

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.420
to help Ministers make fools of themselves?

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.520
Well, I've never met one that needed any help.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.090
That is, of course, the last thing.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:17.850
Well, why did you encourage me to stand up

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.210
and tell Parliament and the press

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.210
that unifying the administration of the countryside

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.330
meant no loss of amenity,

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.330
when apparently I'd just signed the death warrant

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.050
for a whole army of beavers?

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:26.750
Badges, Minister.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:27.070
What?

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.070
A beavers would have a navy.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:33.250
Why did you let me do it, Humphrey?

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.410
With respect, Minister, I did not encourage you to say that.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:37.290
Yes, you did. The department prepared this.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.120
No loss of amenity, it says.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:40.940
No, Minister, not no loss of amenity.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.650
No significant loss of amenity.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:43.450
Well, same thing.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.490
No, on the contrary, there's all the difference in the world.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.410
Almost anything can be attacked as a loss of amenity,

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:52.350
and almost anything can be defended as not a significant loss of amenity,

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.910
which seems to signify that one should appreciate the significance of significant.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:56.250
Yes.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:59.970
Well, be that as it may, any publicity would be very...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.260
Oh, I think no, Minister.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.560
But if I said there'd be no loss of amenity, then there is a loss of amenity.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:06.300
Perhaps if you'd like to approve this release, Minister,

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:09.140
you would see, in fact, that it does counter all their arguments.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:12.980
It points out that the spinney is merely deregistered, not threatened.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.290
Badgers are very plentiful all over Warwickshire.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.780
And it points out the connection between badgers and brucellosis.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.120
Cannot be regarded as a significant loss of amenity at all, though.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:27.980
So you think this might not get into the national press?

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.970
Well, a few lines on the inside...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.160
page of the Guardian, but nothing to worry about. Actually, it's only the urban middle class who

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:38.800
worry about the preservation of the countryside, because they don't have to live in it.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:46.600
Will that be all, Minister? Yes. No, this may well blow over, but it doesn't answer my basic

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:51.590
question. Which was? Why wasn't I told about it before I made the announcement? There are those

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:59.280
who have argued, and indeed cogently, that on occasion there are some things it is better for

00:08:59.340 --> 00:08:59.980
Minister not

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:06.320
to know. What are you talking about? Minister, your answers in the House and at the press conference

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.460
was superb. You were convincing and convinced. The critics were silenced. But could you have

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:18.020
spoken with the same authority if the ecological pressure group had been abadgering you? But I have

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:23.060
a right to know. I am the people's representative. What right have you got to withhold this information

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:26.160
from me? It's intolerant. It's in your own best interest, Minister. What do you mean there are

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.100
something that is better for a minister not to know.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:29.980
I mean, it's outrageous. It must not...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.580
occur again. It so happens that I've got a plan here for the reorganization of this department

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:39.100
which will ensure that it doesn't. Indeed? Yes, indeed. Now, if I were to have one

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.960
under-secretary and two assistant secretaries reporting directly to me...

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.820
Minister, please. No, just a moment, Humphrey. Let me tell you my plan. The plan is immaterial.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:51.700
Minister, I have something to say to you which you may not like

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.390
to hear. Why should today be any different?

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:59.660
Minister, the traditional allocation

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:05.770
of executive responsibilities has always been so determined as to liberate the ministerial

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:13.010
incumbent from the administrative minutiae by devolving the managerial functions to those whose

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:18.110
experience and qualifications have better formed them for the performance of such humble offices

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:24.730
thereby releasing their political overlords for the more onerous duties and profound deliberations

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:28.450
which are the inevitable concomitant of their exalted position.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.920
What made you think I wouldn't want to hear that?

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:38.980
Well, I thought it might upset you.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.920
How could it? I didn't understand a single word.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:46.640
For sake, for once in your life, put it into plain English.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:51.000
If you insist, you are not here to run this department.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:52.630
Pardon?

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:55.700
You are not here to run this department.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.000
I think I am. The people think I am too.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:10:59.750
With respect, Minister.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:08.440
you are they are wrong and who does run this department i do i see and what am i supposed

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:14.690
to do we've been through all this before make policy get legislation enacted and above all

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:18.830
secure the department's budget in cabinet i sometimes think that the budget is all you

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:23.650
ever really care about it is rather important minister if nobody cared about the budget we

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:29.960
might end up with the department so small that even a minister could run it humphrey i'll be about

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:34.910
a fundamental disagreement about the nature of democracy no minister merely a demarcation

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:42.390
dispute what i mean is that the menial chore of running a department is beneath you your fashion

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:47.880
for a nobler calling well i'm quite prepared to leave the routine paperwork to you but i must

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:52.380
insist on direct access to all information never again do i want to hear that phrase there are

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:56.860
some things it is better for a minister not to know is that clear well minister i do that is an

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.980
order, Humphrey. As you say, Minister, if...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.230
If that is what you really want.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:05.550
Treating cabinet ministers as though they were irresponsible ten-year-olds.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:15.800
Good afternoon.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:17.480
Finish with the Guardian, Daddy.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:18.060
Yeah.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.390
Sorry I haven't got the socialist worker for you.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:25.550
Daddy's pleased to see you down for breakfast, aren't you, Jim?

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:26.850
He hasn't seen you for a while.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:28.350
I was having a lie-in.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:29.960
Better than a...

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:33.880
Why were you so late home last night?

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:37.190
There are some things it's better for a father not to know.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:39.130
Oh, you start.

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:40.050
What?

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:41.350
Nothing.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:43.510
I was out with the trots.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:45.060
Don't worry.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:49.470
Are you going to see a doctor?

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:52.680
Skiites.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:54.740
Oh, trotskiites.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:55.260
Are you?

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:56.680
No, Peter is.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:57.280
Peter?

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:58.680
Yes, Peter.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:02.560
I've only met him about 15 times.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:05.360
Oh, is he the one with the, um, from the...

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:05.700
Yes.

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:07.800
Jim, I need your help today.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:08.500
Oh, what with?

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:13.040
Shopping at the Cash and Carry, the kitchen plug holes blocked, the lawn needs mowing.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.200
Darling, I've got all these boxes to do. It is important.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:16.040
Make them wait.

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:20.950
Honey, dear, may I have escaped your notice, but I'm a cabinet minister, a member of Her Majesty's government.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:22.570
It's a fairly important job.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:26.070
But you have 23,000 civil servants to help you. I haven't.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.970
Play with your memos later. The drains need fix...

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.940
Oh, Lucy! I've got marmalade all over my cabinet papers.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:39.940
Oh, my God. Oh, Lord, I've got butter on them.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:40.720
Get a cloth.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:45.790
Lucy! Not in Whitehall now, you know.

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:49.670
Yes, Minister. Certainly, Minister. Just as you say, Minister.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:51.650
Please may I lick your boots for you, Minister?

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:53.850
Lucy, darling, that's not fair.

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:56.730
Those civil servants may be always kowtowing to Daddy,

00:13:56.870 --> 00:13:58.370
but they never take any notice of him.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:05.050
But have you now? I've just won a very significant victory in my department.

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:05.990
Look at all those boxes.

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:08.420
That's what I mean.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:11.300
For a short time you were getting the better of Humphrey.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.020
Now they've done a snow job on you again.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:14.660
Not at all. I asked for all this.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:15.290
Why?

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:21.020
Well, yesterday, in so many words, Humphrey said there are some things it's better for a minister not to know.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.540
Which means that he's withholding things from me.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:25.180
Who knows? Probably important things.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:28.800
So I have insisted that I am told everything that goes on in my department.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.450
How did you get to be a cabinet minister, darling?

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:33.890
You're such a clot.

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:37.040
Don't you see?

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:38.980
You've played right into Humphrey's hands.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.320
He must be utterly delighted.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:44.300
You've given him an open invitation to swamp you with useless information.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:45.160
I don't think so.

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:47.560
Well, what's in that box, for instance?

